        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.595,
            0.049,
            0.847,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Another Peek Beyond the Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "If we follow this new scheme of adding 1 day every four years, but not in the 100th year, the number of calendar days in 1000 years is"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.127,
            0.631,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "3 6 5 2 4 \\times 1 0 = 3 , 6 5 , 2 4 0 \\; \\mathrm { d a y s } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_24_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.15,
            0.847,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": "The number of days the Earth takes to go around the Sun 1000 times is $1 0 0 0 \\times 3 6 5 . 2 4 2 2 = 3 , 6 5 , 2 4 2 . 2 \\mathrm { ~ d a y s } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.194,
            0.497,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "So, there is a difference of 2.2 days."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.218,
            0.848,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": "To bridge this gap, it was decided that every 400th year would be a leap year!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.488,
            0.308,
            0.638,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "Add an extra day every 400th year."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.305,
            0.784,
            0.498
          ],
